From 87d4a664e232dba194d5f760a9af7ddb5132aae1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Gavin John <gavinnjohn@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 16 Sep 2026 01:16:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 053/100] input: give the repeat a proper cushion

The next pass is asked for once the decoders report empty, and whatever the
output is still holding at that moment is the whole cushion the demuxer
restart, the decoder start-up and the scheduler have to fit in. That was
AOUT_MAX_PTS_ADVANCE, 40 ms - enough when everything is prompt, and not much
else. Under load an ogg loop has been seen losing the head of a pass.

Widening aout_DecIsEmpty()'s lead would have done it, but that is the
emptiness contract, and "empty" meaning "a quarter of a second still to play"
is wrong for everyone else who waits for an end - EsOutDel(), the real end of
playback, the timeshift forwarding. So the lead is a parameter instead: the
output reports how much it still holds, the decoder and es_out pass the caller's
lead down, and only the repeat path in the input asks for a wide one.

Nothing is skipped by asking early: the repeat does not flush, and the new
pass is queued behind what is left rather than in place of it.

Measured on a 4 s ogg looped with --repeat on a null sink, over ten runs per
arm: the wait from EOF to "repeating the same input" falls from 1.06 s to
0.85 s, and what the output still holds when the repeat is issued rises from
35-39 ms to 243-249 ms. Marker count, loop period and the gap analysis of a
captured sweep are unchanged.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
